WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.940
More than 20 years ago, Ron Haskins and
Isabel Sawhill at the Brookings

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:13.410
Institution sifted the social science
data and found that anyone who finishes

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:18.270
high school, works full-time, and marries
before having children almost never

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:23.220
experiences poverty. They called this the
"success sequence". What the sequence misses

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:27.779
is that each of these steps is enormously complex. It takes a lot of parental

00:00:27.779 --> 00:00:31.890
attention, energy, and love before a child
crosses the stage at a high school

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:36.360
graduation. Social networks are critical
to finding a full-time job. A happy

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:40.500
marriage is arguably the most complex
challenge of all. Looked at this way, it's

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:45.149
not so much a sequence as a decades-long dialogue between the individual,

00:00:45.149 --> 00:00:49.980
their family and friends, and the
community. Education, work, and marriage

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:55.469
are the marks of a successful life as it
unfolds. We owe Haskins and Sawhill a

00:00:55.469 --> 00:00:59.460
profound thanks for bringing us the
success sequence. The job now is

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.850
developing the strategies for
strengthening families and communities

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:06.560
so that more people find the path and
stay on it.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:07.940
What do you think are the

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:11.900
most important factors in success, and
how can we work together to make the

00:01:11.909 --> 00:01:17.400
promise of America a reality for more
people? Let us know in your comments. Also,

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:21.330
let us know what other topics you'd like
our scholars to cover in 60 seconds, and

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:27.890
be sure to like and subscribe for more
research and videos from AEI.

